{"content_hash": "3febf0bb4747a994", "version_id": 152, "article_id": 16, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Developer builds free AI clones of Adobe apps", "title_zh": "开发者用AI做出免费的Adobe软件克隆版", "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/", "image": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(343, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(21, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "2a1fc9572154"}, "published_date": "2026-10-07", "word_count": 235, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "A developer named Brandon Thomas made free copies of Adobe's popular apps. He used AI to build them. He says they will be as good as Adobe's software soon.", "lead_zh": "一位名叫布兰登·托马斯的开发者做出了Adobe热门软件的免费版本。他用AI来制作这些软件。他说它们很快就会和Adobe的软件一样好。", "preview": "Adobe's Creative Suite is famous but expensive. For years, people have used free open-source apps that do similar things. Now a developer na", "preview_zh": "Adobe的Creative Suite很有名，但很贵。多年来，人们一直使用免费的开源软件来做类似的事情。现在，一位名叫布兰登·托马斯的开发者正在用AI非常接近地复制Adobe的软件。他希望他的软件看起来和用起来都像Adobe的一样。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 142, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/audio/16/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 100.138, "size_bytes": 604656, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/data/versions/152.json", "paragraphs": ["Adobe's Creative Suite is famous but expensive. For years, people have used free open-source apps that do similar things. Now a developer named Brandon Thomas is using AI to copy Adobe's software very closely. He wants his apps to look and work just like Adobe's.", "Artcraft started last year as a tool for artists. It helped people edit AI-made images and videos. Last weekend, it showed seven new open-source apps. These apps copy the design of Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro. Thomas said he used Claude Opus 5.5, an AI tool, to build them in Rust. They also work in a browser.", "The apps are still very early. People on Reddit and Hacker News pointed out many problems. But Thomas promised they will reach full feature parity soon. He first said one month, then changed it to a few months. He said he was tired of Adobe's cancellation fees. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses. Thomas will make money by selling access to Artcraft's AI model.", "Thomas is not the first to use AI for reverse engineering. This means copying how software works without copying its code. It is usually legal. But the apps could face legal trouble if they look too much like Adobe's. Thomas said AI will let developers make free versions of paid software. He wrote, \"Software is over.\" He said he \"one-shotted Photoshop.\""], "paragraphs_zh": ["Adobe的Creative Suite很有名，但很贵。多年来，人们一直使用免费的开源软件来做类似的事情。现在，一位名叫布兰登·托马斯的开发者正在用AI非常接近地复制Adobe的软件。他希望他的软件看起来和用起来都像Adobe的一样。", "Artcraft去年作为一个面向艺术家的工具起步。它帮助人们编辑AI生成的图像和视频。上周末，它展示了七个新的开源软件。这些软件复制了Photoshop、Illustrator、Premiere、Lightroom、After Effects、InDesign和Acrobat Pro的设计。托马斯说，他使用AI工具Claude Opus 5.5，用Rust语言构建了它们。它们也可以在浏览器中运行。", "这些软件还处于非常早期的阶段。Reddit和Hacker News上的人们指出了很多问题。但托马斯承诺它们很快就会达到完整的功能对等。他先说一个月，然后改成了几个月。他说他受够了Adobe的取消费用。这些软件在MIT和Apache许可下是免费的。托马斯将通过出售Artcraft的AI模型访问权来赚钱。", "托马斯不是第一个用AI进行逆向工程的人。逆向工程是指在不复制代码的情况下复制软件的工作方式。这通常是合法的。但如果这些软件看起来太像Adobe的，它们可能会面临法律问题。托马斯说，AI将让开发者能够制作付费软件的免费版本。他写道：“软件已死。”他说他“一击即中”Photoshop。"], "body": "Adobe's Creative Suite is famous but expensive. For years, people have used free open-source apps that do similar things. Now a developer named Brandon Thomas is using AI to copy Adobe's software very closely. He wants his apps to look and work just like Adobe's.\n\nArtcraft started last year as a tool for artists. It helped people edit AI-made images and videos. Last weekend, it showed seven new open-source apps. These apps copy the design of Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro. Thomas said he used Claude Opus 5.5, an AI tool, to build them in Rust. They also work in a browser.\n\nThe apps are still very early. People on Reddit and Hacker News pointed out many problems. But Thomas promised they will reach full feature parity soon. He first said one month, then changed it to a few months. He said he was tired of Adobe's cancellation fees. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses. Thomas will make money by selling access to Artcraft's AI model.\n\nThomas is not the first to use AI for reverse engineering. This means copying how software works without copying its code. It is usually legal. But the apps could face legal trouble if they look too much like Adobe's. Thomas said AI will let developers make free versions of paid software. He wrote, \"Software is over.\" He said he \"one-shotted Photoshop.\"", "body_zh": "Adobe的Creative Suite很有名，但很贵。多年来，人们一直使用免费的开源软件来做类似的事情。现在，一位名叫布兰登·托马斯的开发者正在用AI非常接近地复制Adobe的软件。他希望他的软件看起来和用起来都像Adobe的一样。\n\nArtcraft去年作为一个面向艺术家的工具起步。它帮助人们编辑AI生成的图像和视频。上周末，它展示了七个新的开源软件。这些软件复制了Photoshop、Illustrator、Premiere、Lightroom、After Effects、InDesign和Acrobat Pro的设计。托马斯说，他使用AI工具Claude Opus 5.5，用Rust语言构建了它们。它们也可以在浏览器中运行。\n\n这些软件还处于非常早期的阶段。Reddit和Hacker News上的人们指出了很多问题。但托马斯承诺它们很快就会达到完整的功能对等。他先说一个月，然后改成了几个月。他说他受够了Adobe的取消费用。这些软件在MIT和Apache许可下是免费的。托马斯将通过出售Artcraft的AI模型访问权来赚钱。\n\n托马斯不是第一个用AI进行逆向工程的人。逆向工程是指在不复制代码的情况下复制软件的工作方式。这通常是合法的。但如果这些软件看起来太像Adobe的，它们可能会面临法律问题。托马斯说，AI将让开发者能够制作付费软件的免费版本。他写道：“软件已死。”他说他“一击即中”Photoshop。", "text": "Adobe's Creative Suite is famous but expensive. For years, people have used free open-source apps that do similar things. Now a developer named Brandon Thomas is using AI to copy Adobe's software very closely. He wants his apps to look and work just like Adobe's. Artcraft started last year as a tool for artists. It helped people edit AI-made images and videos. Last weekend, it showed seven new open-source apps. These apps copy the design of Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro. Thomas said he used Claude Opus 5.5, an AI tool, to build them in Rust. They also work in a browser. The apps are still very early. People on Reddit and Hacker News pointed out many problems. But Thomas promised they will reach full feature parity soon. He first said one month, then changed it to a few months. He said he was tired of Adobe's cancellation fees. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses. Thomas will make money by selling access to Artcraft's AI model. Thomas is not the first to use AI for reverse engineering. This means copying how software works without copying its code. It is usually legal. But the apps could face legal trouble if they look too much like Adobe's. Thomas said AI will let developers make free versions of paid software. He wrote, \"Software is over.\" He said he \"one-shotted Photoshop.\"", "vocab": [{"word": "developer", "pos": "noun", "zh": "开发者", "note": "指编写软件的人"}, {"word": "open-source", "pos": "adj", "zh": "开源的", "note": "指源代码公开，任何人都可以免费使用和修改"}, {"word": "app", "pos": "noun", "zh": "应用程序", "note": "application的缩写"}, {"word": "copy", "pos": "verb", "zh": "复制", "note": "这里指模仿或克隆"}, {"word": "feature", "pos": "noun", "zh": "功能", "note": "软件能做的事情"}, {"word": "parity", "pos": "noun", "zh": "对等，同等", "note": "这里指功能上达到相同水平"}, {"word": "license", "pos": "noun", "zh": "许可证", "note": "法律上允许使用软件的证明"}, {"word": "reverse engineering", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "逆向工程", "note": "通过分析产品来理解其工作原理，通常不复制代码"}], "title_original": "“Software is over”: Bold AI developer takes aim at Adobe with open source clones", "summary_original": "Opus-built Creative Cloud alternatives are ambitious, free, and nowhere near finished.", "body_original": "If it quacks like adobe\n“Software is over”: Bold AI developer takes aim at Adobe with open source clones\nOpus-built Creative Cloud alternatives are ambitious, free, and nowhere near finished.\nKyle Orland\n–\nOct 7, 2026 5:56 pm\n|\n74\nThink that's a screen from Photoshop? Look again...\nCredit:\nArtcraft\nThink that's a screen from Photoshop? Look again...\nCredit:\nArtcraft\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nFor a while, those hoping to\navoid the difficulty and expense of dealing with Adobe’s Creative Suite\nhave been able to use\nseveral free and/or open-source alternatives\nthat replicate some or all of those capabilities. Now, one developer is using AI-powered reverse engineering to mimic the look, feel, and functionality of Adobe’s well-known software with what he hopes will soon be perfect fidelity.\nArtcraft\ngot its start\nlast year\nas a “controllable AI for artists” that allows for precise post-generation editing of AI-crafted images and videos. Over the weekend, though, the brand\ndebuted its pivot\nto\na suite of seven open source apps\nrecreating the user interface and tools found in Adobe’s Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro.\nIn\nannouncing those new apps on Reddit\nearlier this week,\nsoftware developer Brandon Thomas\nsaid he used Anthropic’s Claude Opus 5.5 to create “clean-room replacements” for Adobe’s closed source software in Rust (with WebAssembly versions available for use in a browser). And while commenters\non Hacker News\nand\nelsewhere\nhave\ntaken pains\nto point out the new software’s many current shortcomings, Thomas make the grandiose promise that\nthe current “super early alpha” state\nwill soon lead to something just as functional as anything Adobe has ever put out.\n“We’re going to reach 100% [feature] parity within a month,” Thomas\nstated on Reddit\nof his plans to quickly squash bugs and add features with the help of community reports. Days later,\non Hacker News\n, he amended that position slightly to say that “99% parity will take a while, but I’m sure it’ll be measured in months and not years.”\nIs software over?\nThomas, who says he has about a decade of experience working on large-scale Rust projects,\nwrote on Hacker News\nthat he was inspired to create his own open source Adobe software alternatives after being “bit by the dark pattern ‘cancellation fee’ one too many times.” Development of the totally free options offered here under MIT/Apache licenses will be funded, Thomas\nwrote\n, by selling token-based access to\nthe pre-existing Artcraft visual AI model and IDE\n, which is offered as an imagery-generation option inside the apps alongside third-party models. This revenue stream will help ensure these apps aren’t “going to be some orphaned small-team project,” Thomas wrote. “We have a team building popular software for filmmakers, so supporting this is fully funded.”\nThomas is the\nlatest in a long line\nof coders using AI tools to speed up the\nlong-standing but labor-intensive practice\nof reverse engineering (i.e., replicating the functionality of a piece of software without directly copying the code itself). While that practice has\ngenerally been found legally acceptable\n, the ArtCraft apps\ncould still face legal trouble\nif their “trade dress” too closely mimics Adobe’s familiar appearance and interface.\nAfter coding exclusively with AI tools since February, Thomas\nsaid\nhe foresees a near future when everyday developers can re-create open source versions of popular paid software to “take back the internet and own tech forever.”\n“Now you can just create 1:1 functional equivalents, clean room, that are highly performant and cross-platform. Nothing is stopping you,” Thomas wrote. “We’ll replace Google Search, we’ll build our own smartphones, we’ll build better infra. We’ll remove all the ads and de-enshittify the entire tech world.”\n“Software is over,” he gloated in\nan earlier comment\n, claiming to have “one-shotted Photoshop. The whole thing.”\nKyle Orland\nSenior Gaming Editor\nKyle Orland\nSenior Gaming Editor\nKyle Orland has been the Senior Gaming Editor at Ars Technica since 2012, writing primarily about the business, tech, and culture behind video games. He has journalism and computer science degrees from University of Maryland. He once\nwrote a whole book about\nMinesweeper\n.\n74 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:08:45.446425", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Adobe's", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.662, "sm": 100, "em": 662, "cs": 0, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Creative", "s": 0.675, "e": 1.1, "sm": 675, "em": 1100, "cs": 8, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "Suite", "s": 1.113, "e": 1.45, "sm": 1112, "em": 1450, "cs": 17, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "is", "s": 1.462, "e": 1.6, "sm": 1462, "em": 1599, "cs": 23, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "famous", "s": 1.613, "e": 2.025, "sm": 1612, "em": 2025, "cs": 26, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "but", "s": 2.038, "e": 2.188, "sm": 2037, "em": 2187, "cs": 33, "ce": 36, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "expensive", "s": 2.2, "e": 2.975, "sm": 2200, "em": 2975, "cs": 37, "ce": 46, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": ".", "s": 2.975, "e": 3.85, "sm": 2975, "em": 3850, "cs": 46, "ce": 47, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "For", "s": 3.85, "e": 4.075, "sm": 3850, "em": 4075, "cs": 48, "ce": 51, "si": 1}, {"i": 9, "w": "years", "s": 4.088, "e": 4.538, "sm": 4087, "em": 4537, "cs": 52, "ce": 57, "si": 1}, {"i": 10, "w": ",", "s": 4.538, "e": 4.787, "sm": 4537, "em": 4787, "cs": 57, "ce": 58, "si": 1}, {"i": 11, "w": "people", "s": 4.787, "e": 5.125, "sm": 4787, "em": 5125, "cs": 59, "ce": 65, "si": 1}, {"i": 12, "w": "have", "s": 5.138, "e": 5.275, "sm": 5137, "em": 5275, "cs": 66, "ce": 70, "si": 1}, {"i": 13, "w": "used", "s": 5.287, "e": 5.6, "sm": 5287, "em": 5600, "cs": 71, "ce": 75, "si": 1}, {"i": 14, "w": "free", "s": 5.612, "e": 5.862, "sm": 5612, "em": 5862, "cs": 76, "ce": 80, "si": 1}, {"i": 15, "w": "open-source", "s": 5.875, "e": 6.5, "sm": 5875, "em": 6500, "cs": 81, "ce": 92, "si"